    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `generate_ignored_nodes`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `generate_ignored_nodes` function is responsible for generating a sequence of nodes that should be ignored during formatting, based on the presence of a `# fmt: off` comment. It takes three parameters: `leaf`, which is a `Leaf` node in the syntax tree; `comment`, a `ProtoComment` object representing the comment; and `mode`, which is a `Mode` object that likely contains configuration settings for the formatter. The function returns an iterator of nodes (`LN`) that should be ignored.\n\nThe function first checks if the comment contains a `# fmt: skip` directive, in which case it delegates to `_generate_ignored_nodes_from_fmt_skip` to handle the specific logic for skipping nodes. If not, it finds the container node of the `leaf` and iterates through its children and siblings, checking for `# fmt: on` directives. If a `# fmt: on` is found, the function stops generating ignored nodes and returns. It handles cases where `# fmt: on` is placed at different bracket levels or right after an indentation level, ensuring that the formatting does not fail due to invalid use of the directives. If no matching `# fmt: on` is found at the same level, it keeps the `DEDENT` node to maintain the correct indentation structure.\n\nThe function relies on several helper functions: `_contains_fmt_skip_comment`, `container_of`, `is_fmt_on`, `children_contains_fmt_on`, and `_generate_ignored_nodes_from_fmt_skip`. It does not raise exceptions but uses early returns to handle different scenarios, such as finding a `# fmt: on` directive or reaching the end of the syntax tree. The function has no side effects; it only generates and returns a sequence of nodes to be ignored.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
